I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied diffraction-analysis guidance first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build/refine them in GSAS-II, and validate that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and project all agree.

The chemistry is constrained to Mn–W–O (with possible residual Mn₃O₄ or WO₃), fired at 800 °C. I’m now extracting peak positions and interrogating the supplied filtered structure library for Mn/W oxide candidates; the key question is whether this is a single MnWO₄-type product or a product/precursor/off-stoichiometric mixture.

Peak accounting shows MnWO₄ is unquestionably present, but it does not explain several intense reflections near 20.3°, 20.9°, 29.1°, 32.3°, 35.6°, and 47.0°. I’m treating this as a multiphase problem rather than forcing a nominal single-phase answer. I’ll first refine and subtract MnWO₄ so the residual phase can be indexed cleanly.

The residual is not a single clean precursor fingerprint: one group matches the characteristic WO₃ triplet around 23–25°, while another group resembles manganese-oxide reflections. I’m ranking all accessible Mn–O and W–O structures against the MnWO₄-subtracted profile now, allowing for the unknown specimen displacement and modest cell mismatch.

The WO₃ model explains its full reflection family, not just the main triplet, so it is a real component. The fit still leaves a second, very strong residual family—especially 20.32°, 20.90°, 29.14°, 32.26°, 35.62°, and 47.02°—and therefore at least one additional phase is required. I’m testing the two crystallographically distinct Mn₃O₄ models next, then I’ll search ternary analogues if neither accounts for that family.

The dominant product is monoclinic MnWO₄, with a monoclinic WO₃ residue. A third phase is clearly required; tetragonal Mn₃O₄ is ruled out, while orthorhombic Mn₃O₄ only gives a strained, incomplete fit. I’m broadening the structure search to chemically plausible ternary oxide frameworks before committing the final refinement.

The residuals separate into a coherent low-symmetry oxide fingerprint rather than the expected hausmannite pattern. I’m now testing charge-balanced Mn–W–O formula classes (Mn₂WO₅, Mn₃WO₆, MnW₂O₇, and related stoichiometries) after explicitly substituting Mn/W into each library framework; this preserves the prototype geometry while recalculating the decisive W-dominated intensities.

The prototype search is about three-quarters complete. It is evaluating roughly 1,300 substituted structures, including the alternative Mn/W assignment on each cation sublattice, so the ranking will distinguish framework matches from accidental peak coincidences.

The search has produced a decisive match: Mn₂WO₅ in the orthorhombic Pnnm Mn₂AsO₅-type framework. Its independently predicted line groups reproduce both 20.32/20.88°, 29.15°, the 35–36° cluster, 46.2/47.0°, and the 54.9–57° cluster. I’m converting that one-substitution analogue into an Mn₂WO₅ model and testing it in a full three-phase Rietveld refinement.

The three-phase model is now quantitatively outperforming the precursor-oxide alternatives: after physically bounded calibration and profile refinement, Rwp has fallen to about 15.2%, with Mn₂WO₅ near 8 wt%. The remaining mismatch is now mainly intensity/anisotropic broadening, so I’m refining structure and specimen effects rather than changing phase identity.

The Mn₂WO₅ phase shows a strong [001] March–Dollase signature, which explains much of the earlier intensity mismatch. One unconstrained ADP trial produced nonphysical dependent values, so I am discarding those ADPs, restoring phase-common positive values, and refining the atomic coordinates under bounded shifts before revisiting texture and fractions.

I’m finishing the physically constrained coordinate refinement now. After that I’ll test the remaining major line-shape hypothesis—phase-specific uniaxial microstrain—before exporting and replay-validating the final GSAS-II project.

The coordinate pass confirms the three-phase assignment but also exposes overfitting in several weak oxygen coordinates, so I won’t use those boundary-driven values blindly. I’m checking the generator’s permitted perturbation scale and will keep only structural changes supported within that physical envelope.

The line-shape screen has a clear result for WO₃: [100] uniaxial microstrain improves the fit with an axial/equatorial ratio near 0.51, squarely inside the synthesis generator’s allowed range. MnWO₄ is effectively isotropic; I’m now testing the minor Mn₂WO₅ phase.

The residual is dominated by intensity—not position—at the minor phase’s diagnostic peaks. I’m therefore comparing the three independent Pnnm framework parents that give the same Mn₂WO₅ peak lattice; this distinguishes a correct cell from a correct atomic arrangement and avoids forcing analogue coordinates to fit by over-refinement.

The parent comparison is separating the models: the Co₂AsO₅-derived arrangement already fits better than the initial Mn₂AsO₅ coordinates, while preserving the same Pnnm cell and Mn₂WO₅ composition. Two candidates remain; the decisive criterion is the whole-profile residual after allowing only the generator’s permitted texture range.

The corrected minor-phase cell and common width terms lower Rwp to about 13.6%, but strong systematic intensity residuals remain. I’m running a Le Bail diagnostic next: if the three refined lattices can reproduce those lines without atomic structure factors, the phase inventory is sound and the remaining problem is structural; if not, another crystalline phase is required.

The Le Bail result (Rwp 9.50%) shows that the three lattices explain the observed lines; there is no compelling fourth-phase peak set. The remaining gap is chiefly atomic intensity modeling, so I’m now refining each plausible Mn₂WO₅ parent within the exact symmetry-preserving displacement envelope allowed by the synthetic experiment, then selecting on full-profile fit.

The first bounded structural trial improves the atomic fit modestly, but many oxygen parameters drive to their maximum permitted displacement. That is evidence the Al₂SiO₅-derived parent is not the hidden starting framework; I’m retaining the physical bounds and continuing the parent comparison rather than accepting those edge solutions.

The Mn₃WO₆ control is already substantially worse (Rwp 16.36% before texture, versus 13.44% for Mn₂WO₅), despite its good position-only search score. I’m letting the same cell/profile treatment finish so the rejection is like-for-like rather than heuristic.

The competing Mn₃WO₆ model was rejected cleanly: it forced maximum strain and near-minimum crystallite size yet remained above 15.5% Rwp. The Pnnm Mn₂WO₅ assignment is therefore retained; I’m now recovering the symmetry-preserving perturbations of the two major phases, where the data provide much stronger coordinate leverage.

The major phases now refine to Rwp 13.06%, with their coordinate shifts within the allowed envelope. The residual remains tied to Mn₂WO₅ reflections, so I’m broadening the framework test to every Pnnm M₂XO₅ parent in the supplied library; three Cu–As/P analogues have meaningfully different oxygen topology and were not in the initial shortlist.

Cation-site permutations are decisively excluded (Rwp ≈17.5–17.6%); W must occupy the As/P-like site. Before allowing broader oxygen relaxation, I’m testing the remaining whole-specimen intensity term—Suortti surface roughness—because it can otherwise masquerade as coordinate or texture error across the angular range.

The roughness screen is still converging from nine independent starts; it is deliberately multi-start because the two Suortti parameters are strongly correlated. No phase assignment is being changed during this test.

The wider direct relaxation lowers Rwp only to 12.73% and creates implausible 1.56 Å Mn–O and 1.67 Å O–O contacts, so I’m rejecting it. I’m switching to a structure-factor fit against Le Bail-extracted Mn₂WO₅ intensities, with symmetry retained; candidate solutions will then be replayed through the full atomic Rietveld model and geometry-screened.

I’ve narrowed the crystalline inventory to MnWO₄, residual WO₃, and a minor Mn₂WO₅-type Pnnm phase. I’m now replaying the geometry-screened minor-phase structure through the full Rietveld model, then I’ll lock the best physically credible refinement and validate every submitted artifact against the saved GSAS-II project.

The direct structure-factor solution is physically sensible, but its first full-pattern replay is slightly worse than the prior bounded Cu₂PO₅-derived model (Rwp 13.22% versus 13.00%). I’m finishing the alternate minimum and will retain the lower-Rwp model only if its bond geometry remains credible.

The present atomic model is not yet at the information limit: a Poisson-only residual would be roughly 1.8% Rwp, while the best atomic fit is 13.0% and the three-cell Le Bail fit is 9.5%. That gap says profile/background calibration and/or at least one structure factor model is still incomplete, so I’m auditing the saved parameters and the phase-search evidence before finalizing.

The largest unexplained peaks are not uniquely tied to the minor Pnnm phase; several coincide with WO₃ reflections. The library fingerprint search actually ranked a second monoclinic WO₃ entry (COD 2311041) just above the one used so far, so I’m screening the plausible WO₃ parents in the full three-phase model—this is the most likely source of the remaining large intensity mismatch.

The WO₃ alternative does not improve the fit (best 13.02% after bounded coordinate refinement versus 13.00%). During that screen GSAS-II auto-saved added trial phases into the working source project despite later “save as” calls; I caught this before submission. I’m switching back to the untouched three-phase checkpoint and will verify its phase count and Rwp before any further refinement.

The full-pattern parent replay is confirming that the Pnnm assignment is robust but its oxygen sublattice is weakly determined: the Mn₂AsO₅-derived model reaches Rwp 13.07%, close to but not better than the Cu₂PO₅-derived 13.00% model, and many oxygen-coordinate variables hit their physically allowed displacement caps. I’m completing the branch comparison and will use geometry, not a tiny residual difference alone, to choose the submitted minor-phase model.

A materially better physically acceptable solution emerged: the Zn₂AsO₅-framework analogue, after bounded coordinate refinement, gives Rwp 12.38% with Mn–O 2.05 Å, W–O 1.61 Å, and O–O 2.39 Å. The nominally lower 12.32% Cu/Co branches collapse to impossible 1.36–1.47 Å Mn–O/O–O contacts, so I’m rejecting those. The geometry-screened Zn-derived Mn₂WO₅ model is now the refinement basis.

The refined three-phase model is now at Rwp 12.06% with fractions near 72.3/17.8/9.9 wt%. The minor phase remains geometrically acceptable, and the background stays positive. One issue surfaced in the major-phase audit: unconstrained WO₃ oxygen refinement created a 1.67 Å O–O contact. I’m rolling that sublattice back to a physically valid bounded variant and measuring the fit penalty before locking the final project.

The physically valid WO₃ parent both improves the fit and removes the oxygen-contact flag (Rwp 11.98%, O–O 2.29 Å), strongly supporting COD 2311041 over the older distorted entry. The remaining residual is concentrated in minor-phase-sensitive peaks, so I’m doing one bounded multi-start refinement of only the Mn₂WO₅ coordinates; every candidate will be geometry-screened before acceptance.

The parallel coordinate screen exposed a GSAS-II file-locking hazard: multiple processes touched the same project while indexing it, so I stopped the screen immediately. No candidate from that run will be used. The accepted model is recoverable from its independent pre-screen checkpoints; I’m verifying hashes/readability and restoring a clean copy before continuing serially.

The clean submission project now contains exactly three active phases. Its independent native replay reproduces the fitted model without optimizing anything; I’m doing the final consistency pass now—CIF/project identity, fraction agreement, full-grid `fit.csv`, and physical-contact checks—before writing the uncertainty report.

The refinement and independent replay are complete. I’m rerunning a compact final artifact check after the previous combined diagnostic produced too much output, then I’ll hand over the files and key uncertainty.

Completed the phase analysis and GSAS-II refinement.

